# NestJS development best practices

Prefer [docs.nestjs.com](https://docs.nestjs.com/) and the [migration guide](https://docs.nestjs.com/migration-guide) over blog summaries. **Nest 11** requires **Node.js 20+** (v16/v18 dropped).

## Architecture and modules

- Organize by **feature modules**; keep **single responsibility** for providers. Avoid **circular dependencies** (forward refs only when necessary).
- Use **constructor injection** only; prefer **interfaces + injection tokens** when swapping implementations.
- Understand provider **scopes** (`DEFAULT`, `REQUEST`, `TRANSIENT`) before making services request-scoped.
- **Nest 11:** Dynamic modules with identical deep-equal configs may no longer merge—reuse **one** `DynamicModule` instance (e.g. assign `const m = X.forRoot({})` and import `m` everywhere) if you relied on deduplication.

## HTTP layer

- **Validate all external input:** global `ValidationPipe` with sensible defaults is the documented baseline.

```typescript
app.useGlobalPipes(
  new ValidationPipe({
    whitelist: true,
    forbidNonWhitelisted: true,
    transform: true,
  }),
);
```

- Use **concrete DTO classes** with **class-validator** (not interfaces/types-only imports for DTOs—metadata is lost). Use **`ParseIntPipe`**, **`ParseUUIDPipe`**, **`ParseDatePipe`** (v11+), **`ParseArrayPipe`** where appropriate.
- Put auth in **Guards**, cross-cutting behavior in **Interceptors**, errors in **Exception Filters**—not scattered `try/catch` in every controller.

## Security and config

- Do not trust `req.body` without validation. Use **`ConfigModule`** / `ConfigService` for secrets; **Nest 11** changes how **`get()`** merges env vs factories—see migration guide (`skipProcessEnv` for tests).
- Rate limiting, CORS, Helmet (or Fastify equivalents), and JWT/session patterns belong in **documented** Nest patterns, not ad hoc middleware only.

## Lifecycle and shutdown

- Implement **`OnModuleDestroy` / `OnApplicationShutdown`** for DB pools, timers, and streams. Call **`app.enableShutdownHooks()`** so **SIGTERM** (e.g. Kubernetes) runs teardown—see [lifecycle](https://docs.nestjs.com/fundamentals/lifecycle-events).
- **Nest 11:** Order of **termination** lifecycle hooks **reversed**—re-verify ordering when upgrading.
- **Request-scoped** providers do **not** receive app **init/shutdown** hooks in the same way as singletons.

## Nest 11 platform notes

- **Express 5 / Fastify 5:** Wildcards need **names** (e.g. `users/*splat`, not bare `/*`). Review route strings—see [migration guide](https://docs.nestjs.com/migration-guide).
- **`CacheModule`:** **cache-manager v6** + **Keyv**—follow the cache section in the migration guide when upgrading.
- **`IntrinsicException`:** Use when an exception must **not** be auto-logged by the framework (sensitive paths).
- **Logging:** `ConsoleLogger` supports **JSON** output for containers (`json: true`).
- **Microservices:** **`unwrap()`**, **`on()`**, **`status`** on client/service refs for observability and native client access—see [microservices basics](https://docs.nestjs.com/microservices/basics).

## Testing and quality

- Keep controllers **thin**; unit-test services and use e2e tests for HTTP contracts. Align with your workspace’s chosen runner (Jest is common; verify **Nest CLI** and **@nestjs/cli** version for defaults).

## Errors and observability

- Throw **HTTP exceptions** from `@nestjs/common` for expected failures; use **exception filters** for consistent response shape in production (avoid leaking stack traces).
